import{_ as s,C as o,o as i,c as r,a2 as t,E as a}from"./chunks/framework.9yf7n3zg.js";const h="/guide/media/sentence-log-column.webp",d="/guide/media/sentence-log-desk.webp",k=JSON.parse('{"title":"Sentence log","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"guide/sentence-log.md","filePath":"guide/sentence-log.md","lastUpdated":1792171429000}'),c={name:"guide/sentence-log.md"};function l(g,e,p,u,w,m){const n=o("Clip");return i(),r("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=t('<h1 id="sentence-log" tabindex="-1">Sentence log <a class="header-anchor" href="#sentence-log" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Sentence log&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Many writers keep a file of the sentences they cut, because deleting a good sentence is the hardest thing in revision. Leathercase keeps that file for you. Once per sitting it compares each sentence as it was when you sat down with what it became, and the sentences you rewrote or cut go into the story&#39;s sentence log, with where they were, the day, and what replaced them. You never have to save a copy before you cut. The log only reads your files; nothing in it is ever written back into a chapter.</p><p><img src="'+h+'" alt="The side column of the manuscript with the Sentence log section open: a struck sentence, Rewritten Oct 6, Now: and the new sentence, and Copy" width="375" height="344" loading="lazy"></p><h2 id="what-a-sitting-is" tabindex="-1">What a sitting is <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-a-sitting-is" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What a sitting is&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Leathercase cannot see your cursor; it sees your files each time you save them. So it compares a sentence once per sitting, not at every save:</p><ul><li>A sitting begins when you open the story in Leathercase. The text at that moment is the starting point.</li><li>Each time you save in your writing app, Leathercase reads the story again and moves the sitting on. Nothing is logged yet, so a save in the middle of a word never logs anything, and a sentence rewritten three times in one sitting is logged once, as it was before you started.</li><li>The sitting ends when you close the story, or after half an hour with no change, or when the day turns. Then Leathercase compares the start with the end and logs what changed. An app left open for days still logs each day&#39;s work on its own day.</li></ul><p>Work done while Leathercase was closed is not lost. Leathercase keeps, for each story, the text the last sitting ended with. When you open the story next, it compares that with the files as they are now and logs whatever changed in between, in any software, on that day. The same happens after a crash or a quit before the log was written.</p><h2 id="which-scene-you-worked-on" tabindex="-1">Which scene you worked on <a class="header-anchor" href="#which-scene-you-worked-on" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Which scene you worked on&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Each time Leathercase reads the story, it also notes which scenes changed and by how many words, including what you changed while it was closed. These notes go to the story&#39;s writing log, not to the sentence log. They are there so that an agent you connected can tell you the scene you worked on last. It says how it knows: from Leathercase&#39;s own record, or, for work from before Leathercase first read the story, from the date of the chapter&#39;s file. A change found when the story opens is noted as made some time after Leathercase last read it, since it cannot know when. See <a href="/guide/reference/suite-files">Files Leathercase writes</a>.</p><h2 id="what-counts-as-a-change" tabindex="-1">What counts as a change <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-counts-as-a-change" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What counts as a change&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Not every edit is a revision. Leathercase logs a sentence only when it was rewritten or cut:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>You did</th><th>Logged?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fixed a typo (&quot;teh&quot; to &quot;the&quot;), or changed only case or punctuation</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>Changed two words or more, or a quarter of a short sentence&#39;s letters</td><td>Yes, as rewritten, with what replaced it</td></tr><tr><td>Deleted a sentence, or a whole paragraph</td><td>Yes, each sentence as cut</td></tr><tr><td>Moved a sentence to another place, in the same chapter or another one</td><td>No: it was moved, not cut</td></tr><tr><td>Deleted a file whose sentences turn up nowhere else</td><td>Yes, each sentence as cut</td></tr></tbody></table><p>To tell a rewrite from a cut, Leathercase looks for the new sentence most like the old one nearby. If they share enough words in order, the new one replaced the old; if nothing is close enough, the old one was cut and the new one written fresh.</p><h2 id="in-the-manuscript-s-side-column" tabindex="-1">In the manuscript&#39;s side column <a class="header-anchor" href="#in-the-manuscript-s-side-column" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;In the manuscript&#39;s side column&quot;">​</a></h2><p>On the <a href="/guide/guide/manuscript">manuscript</a> page, click a sentence. The side column&#39;s <strong>Sentence log</strong> section, under <strong>Passage</strong>, lists what was logged for that paragraph, newest first. Its hint gives the count, such as <code>2 sentences</code>, and the section opens by itself when there is something to show.</p><p>Each entry shows:</p><ul><li>The sentence as it was, struck through.</li><li><strong>Rewritten Oct 6</strong> or <strong>Cut Oct 6</strong>: what happened and the day.</li><li><strong>Now:</strong> and the sentence that replaced it, for a rewrite.</li><li><strong>Copy</strong>, which puts the old sentence on the clipboard. The button reads <strong>Copied</strong> after.</li></ul><p>The line under the list says: &quot;Copy puts a sentence on the clipboard. Leathercase never writes it back into your file.&quot; To restore a sentence, copy it and paste it in your own app.</p><p>A paragraph shows the sentences logged at its number in the chapter, and any rewrite whose new sentence is in it now, so a rewrite stays with its paragraph even after paragraphs above it were added or cut.</p><p>With nothing logged for the paragraph the section says: &quot;Nothing logged here. A sentence you rewrite or cut in this paragraph is kept when the sitting ends.&quot;</p>',20)),a(n,{name:"sentence-log-copy",caption:"Clicking a rewritten sentence in chapter one, then Copy in the Sentence log section"}),e[1]||(e[1]=t('<h2 id="on-the-desk" tabindex="-1">On the Desk <a class="header-anchor" href="#on-the-desk" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;On the Desk&quot;">​</a></h2><p><a href="/guide/guide/desk">The Desk</a> counts the day&#39;s logged sentences as revision work. Under <strong>Revision work</strong> it lists each story with sentences logged today, such as <strong>The Lamp at Ferrol · 4 sentences logged today</strong>, with the line &quot;Rewritten and cut sentences, kept in each story&#39;s sentence log.&quot; Click a story to open it. A day spent cutting and rewriting shows as work done, even when the word count went down.</p><p><img src="'+d+'" alt="The Desk with its Revision work section listing The Lamp at Ferrol with 1 sentence logged today" width="660" height="166" loading="lazy"></p><h2 id="marking-a-moment" tabindex="-1">Marking a moment <a class="header-anchor" href="#marking-a-moment" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Marking a moment&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A mark names a point in the log, so you can find &quot;everything I cut after the dinner rewrite&quot; later. With a story open:</p><ol><li>Run <strong>Mark the sentence log</strong> from the command palette (<code>Ctrl+K</code>, or <code>Cmd+K</code> on macOS). The find field in the title bar opens with <code>mark: </code> typed in.</li><li>Type the name of the moment, for example <code>mark: before the rewrite</code>.</li><li>Pick <strong>Mark the sentence log: before the rewrite</strong> and press <code>Enter</code>.</li></ol><p>You can also type <code>mark: </code> and a name into the find field (<code>Ctrl/Cmd+O</code>) yourself. Nothing changes on screen: the mark is written to the log, and in the log as rendered below it is a heading, <code>### before the rewrite</code>, under the day&#39;s heading. Everything logged after it sits above it, since the log reads newest first.</p>',7)),a(n,{name:"sentence-log-mark",caption:"Running Mark the sentence log from the palette and naming the mark before the rewrite"}),e[2]||(e[2]=t(`<h2 id="what-the-log-looks-like" tabindex="-1">What the log looks like <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-the-log-looks-like" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What the log looks like&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Rendered as markdown, which is how an agent you connected reads it, the log reads newest first, one heading per day, one entry per sentence:</p><div class="language-markdown vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">markdown</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-light-font-weight:bold;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;--shiki-dark-font-weight:bold;">## 2026-10-06</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#E36209;--shiki-dark:#FFAB70;">-</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> [[Act I - The Dark Lamp/01 The Night the Lamp Went Out.md#The cottage|01 The Night the Lamp Went Out › The cottage]] ¶ 2</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#E36209;--shiki-dark:#FFAB70;">  -</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> ~~She had slept under that light for twenty-two years.~~</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#E36209;--shiki-dark:#FFAB70;">  -</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> She had slept under that light all her life.</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-light-font-weight:bold;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;--shiki-dark-font-weight:bold;">### before the rewrite</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Each entry says where the sentence was (the chapter, the scene and the paragraph), strikes the old sentence through, and gives what replaced it under it. A struck sentence with nothing under it was cut. A <code>###</code> heading is a mark you named. The rendering opens with a short paragraph in the story&#39;s language.</p><h2 id="where-it-is-kept" tabindex="-1">Where it is kept <a class="header-anchor" href="#where-it-is-kept" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Where it is kept&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The log is one of Leathercase&#39;s logs, kept in the story&#39;s <code>suite</code> folder under <code>suite/logs/sentences/</code>, never among your chapters:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>File</th><th>What it holds</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>suite/logs/sentences/2026-10-06.&lt;machine&gt;.jsonl</code></td><td>The log itself: one line per logged sentence or mark, one file per day and per computer.</td></tr><tr><td><code>suite/Sentences.json</code></td><td>The text the last sitting ended with, so the next sitting can compare. Never shown, and not a second copy of your book: only what the log compares.</td></tr><tr><td><code>suite/logs/writing/</code></td><td>The writing log: the words added and cut at each reading, and the scenes that changed.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Each computer writes only its own file, so a story synced between a desktop and a laptop keeps one log without two machines ever writing the same file. The log is kept forever; nothing is pruned.</p><p>A <code>Sentence log.md</code> from an earlier version of Leathercase (and its yearly files, such as <code>Sentence log 2026.md</code>) is read once, its entries taken into the log, and then left as it was. Leathercase no longer writes <code>Sentence log.md</code>, and it never reads either file as part of your story.</p><h2 id="see-also" tabindex="-1">See also <a class="header-anchor" href="#see-also" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;See also&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><a href="/guide/guide/manuscript">Manuscript</a></li><li><a href="/guide/guide/desk">The Desk</a></li><li><a href="/guide/reference/suite-files">Files Leathercase writes</a></li><li><a href="/guide/guide/connect-an-agent">Connect an agent</a></li><li><a href="/guide/guide/lenses">Lenses and style checks</a></li></ul>`,11))])}const f=s(c,[["render",l]]);export{k as __pageData,f as default};
